  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure centered over the eastern Great Lakes will migrate\neast this evening and crest over the area by Tuesday morning.\nGusty NW winds will diminish this evening as the gradient\nsoftens and will likely go calm overnight. The combination of\ncalm winds and clear skies will optimize radiational cooling\nleading to lows in the mid to low 30s across the north to lows\nin the low 40s near the coast and southern NH. Frost Advisories\nremain unchanged since the last forecast with localized patchy\nfrost elsewhere in favorable radiational cooling spots. Valley\nfog will be likely tonight.\n\nHigh pressure will provide fair weather Tuesday through\nWednesday. A cool air mass will remain in place limiting highs\nto the upper 60s to low 70s Tuesday. Strong ridging over the\nSoutheast will attempt to push northward Tuesday into Wednesday\nbringing warmer air into the region. This will result in lows\nTuesday night mainly in the 40s and highs on Wednesday pushing\nwell into the 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nIn the wake of broad cyclonic flow exiting northern Quebec \nWednesday, a wide upper jet develops through central Ontario. \nThis strong zonal flow north of record max 500mb heights across \nthe central and eastern CONUS will be responsible for a lot of \nthe momentum entering northern New England mid to late week. \n\nThe upper flow prevents a lot of deeper moisture from advancing\nnorth, but there will be a disturbance rapidly exiting the \neastern Great Lakes Thursday that could make use of some of this\nmore humid airmass. Most model guidance introduces the chance \nof showers Thursday afternoon and evening across a portion of \nthe forecast area, but there are many outcomes relating to \nfootprint and rainfall totals. While PWATs climb, there is some \nhesitancy to claim the chance for heavy rainfall at this time. \nThis is mainly due to how progressive the IVT plume is and the \nlack of substantial CAPE for convective enhancement. For now \nit's likely it will either be a period of rain passing Thursday \nafternoon, or some more intermittent showers. \n\nBehind the passing cold front, NW winds become unidirectional \nfor Friday. It will be more breezy, but temps may not sway too \nmuch cooler than Thursday as warm/moist airmass remains \nsteadfast to the south, and dry air should promote mostly sunny \nconditions. Cooler temperatures will be more likely into next \nweekend as moisture rounds the top of high pressure to the \nsouth. This will lead to an increase in clouds, as well as \nrainfall chances.",
